1
00:00:00,099 --> 00:00:01,620
Train tracks sit on a bed of sharp,

2
00:00:01,879 --> 00:00:03,939
angular gravel, and it is there on purpose.

3
00:00:04,219 --> 00:00:06,159
A loaded train puts enormous weight onto a

4
00:00:06,219 --> 00:00:08,179
thin steel rail, and the stones spread that

5
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:10,239
load sideways so the ground underneath does

6
00:00:10,279 --> 00:00:10,879
not sink.

7
00:00:10,979 --> 00:00:12,799
Because they are angular rather than rounded,

8
00:00:13,039 --> 00:00:14,839
they lock against each other and stop the track

9
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:15,679
creeping out of line.

10
00:00:16,039 --> 00:00:17,739
Rain drains through the gaps instead of turning

11
00:00:17,779 --> 00:00:18,479
the bed to mud.

12
00:00:18,739 --> 00:00:20,239
And the layer soaks up vibration,

13
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,180
which is why a passing train shakes the ground

14
00:00:22,279 --> 00:00:22,939
less than it should.
